var e=`# CHAPTER 7 — The World Saw\r
\r
While Kim was still on his knees on the annex concrete, the world had already started catching fire.\r
\r
Not everywhere at once. In waves. As always.\r
\r
---\r
\r
The Yohualticit tower had ninety floors.\r
\r
Yohu, for anyone under thirty. The company that had invented Lutas de Ilusão and, with it, become the most powerful corporation on the planet. Hologram floating in the black marble corridor. Glass walkway over thirty meters of empty space. People in suits walking fast, silent.\r
\r
Until all the alarms in all departments went off at once.\r
\r
Red light pulsing through the whole building. Scientist dropping an experiment midway. Programmer taking his hand off the keyboard. Assistant running with tablet over the Persian-carpet floors.\r
\r
In a regional office, far away, a gray-haired engineer adjusted his augmented reality glasses and read what was coming up in front of him.\r
\r
— Global Red Code. — Low voice. — The system detected an anomaly.\r
\r
He read it again, to be sure.\r
\r
— Primordial Blood.\r
\r
No one in the room knew exactly what that meant. Everyone understood the gravity anyway.\r
\r
---\r
\r
At the central headquarters, in the Zairan District, the alert swept through the seventy-fifth floor.\r
\r
*CRITICAL ALERT — PRIMORDIAL CODE DETECTED.*\r
\r
Nakamura, a young curly-haired technician, stood staring at the data with his fingers frozen over the controller.\r
\r
— This is impossible. Primordial Blood. I never thought I'd see this code in my life.\r
\r
Three floors below, in Valloria's virtual reality sector, operators were coming out of the immersion capsules taking off their visors. Supervisor Chen Liu watched the entire wall of screens. The neural graphs of players connected to LDI worldwide had red spikes.\r
\r
— All neural monitors overloading. — A technician, sweating. — Players are reacting to something they shouldn't even be able to feel.\r
\r
---\r
\r
In the Marelia command center, the chief of operations adjusted her glasses.\r
\r
Curly hair tied up any old way. The whole team around her.\r
\r
On the glass table, a map of the digital world. Red dots blinking on four continents, like an open wound.\r
\r
— Someone explain to me how a Legendary code is active on four continents at once. — She looked at each face at the table. — And why all our security systems are screaming like it's the end of the world.\r
\r
An analyst with glasses raised her hand.\r
\r
— Ma'am... the energy patterns are converging on a single point. It's as if something is calling the Primordial Blood.\r
\r
The room went silent. Even the machines seemed to lower their volume.\r
\r
Primordial Blood was legend. Something spoken in low voices, only in the company's most closed circles.\r
\r
— Alpha team, analysis of all epicenters. — The chief. — Beta team, isolate the suspect codes. No one leaves here until we have answers.\r
\r
---\r
\r
On a private island in the South Pacific, David Kronos watched everything.\r
\r
Holographic screens floating in the control room. Black marble wall, floor-to-ceiling glass, the ocean outside. He was reclined in a leather armchair that looked like a throne, impeccable white suit, long black hair tied in a low ponytail. Sun-tanned skin. Steel-blue eyes.\r
\r
In his right hand, a glass of red wine, swirling slowly.\r
\r
On one of the screens a boy's photo appeared. His data flashing red.\r
\r
And below the photo, in scarlet letters:\r
\r
*PRIMORDIAL BLOOD.*\r
\r
David smiled. A smile of triumph and obsession.\r
\r
— Finally. — Almost a whisper. — How I waited for this moment. Primordial Blood within my reach. And this time nothing and no one will stop me from taking it.\r
\r
He raised the glass to the screen, as if toasting fate. Drank the red slowly.\r
\r
— I am the only one worthy of it. The only one who deserves it.\r
\r
For an instant, something ancient shone in his eyes. As if a very old spirit lived in that modern body.\r
\r
— He will be mine. Regardless of the price.\r
\r
The ocean around the island was completely serene.\r
\r
---\r
\r
And the video ran the world.\r
\r
Forums boiling. Social media exploding. Clip of the fight being dissected frame by frame by millions of eyes. Livestreams sprouting every minute, every influencer wanting a piece.\r
\r
Some swore it was a bug. Others, a prodigy. Everyone wanted to know the same thing.\r
\r
Who was Brawler 142536?\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Arenia, Sarah stood slowly from the blue velvet armchair. The apple tea still hot on the table. Her silver bracelets trembling a little on her wrist.\r
\r
She paused the video on the final blow. The silk veil slipped as she leaned forward.\r
\r
It wasn't admiration. It was hunger for a fight.\r
\r
— He isn't invincible. — In her language, to no one.\r
\r
Outside, the call to prayer echoed through the ancient city. She was already putting her strategy together.\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Draymoor, Alex stepped away from the computer and went to the fogged-up window.\r
\r
The usual rain beating against the glass. The tea cooling on the table. The chimneys of the old factories drawing shadows in the gray sky.\r
\r
He had never seen anyone fight with that precision. It was like watching an artist paint with controlled violence.\r
\r
His heart beat to the rhythm of the rain. Fast.\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Thessor, little Mia jumped up from the couch.\r
\r
Nine years old. Still too young to fight in official LDI. Her grandmother making dinner in the kitchen, the smell reaching the living room.\r
\r
She ran to her room, grabbed paper, and started drawing Kim. Next to him, herself. A modern warrior conquering the digital world.\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Wendor, in an isolated cabin in the middle of the snow, Jaret pulled the chair close to the fireplace.\r
\r
Retired veteran. Old, but still broad. Fifteen below outside, the fire crackling. Red and black flannel shirt. Gray beard. Old scars on the backs of his hands. The tablet on his lap.\r
\r
For decades he had thought his glory days were buried under the snow.\r
\r
He watched the fight twice.\r
\r
*Maybe there's still fight left in this old bear.*\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Zylvaron, on the forty-seventh floor of a glass tower, Mikael stopped the investment presentation midway.\r
\r
Air conditioning at twenty-three degrees. Down below, thirty-five. White shirt, silk tie, white orchid on the table.\r
\r
His phone buzzed on the table. *BRIGUENTO 142536 — SDR #1.201.* The speaker went on about projected returns for the next quarter. Chart, analysis, number. It all became a blur.\r
\r
Only one certainty remained.\r
\r
He needed to face that boy.\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Erendale, Isabella stopped in the middle of her ten-step skincare routine.\r
\r
Her fingers, nails decorated, curled until they marked her palm. The school uniform hanging next to the cosmetics. The gamer setup with custom mechanical keyboard.\r
\r
Three years of a specialized gaming prep course. Hundreds of victories. And that boy made her look like a noob.\r
\r
Envy burned.\r
\r
— I'm going to destroy him. — Through her teeth.\r
\r
She was already imagining the revenge going viral.\r
\r
---\r
\r
In Valetis, Tira dried her hands on her apron.\r
\r
The meatballs cooling on the stove. Snow starting to cover the pine trees outside. Eight below on the window thermometer.\r
\r
Homemaker. Mother of two. And, on the afternoons when the kids were at daycare, one of the best LDI strategists no one knew.\r
\r
She opened a spreadsheet and started separating Kim's movements. The ones before the turnaround in one column. The ones after, in the other.\r
\r
The second column stayed almost empty. Nothing he did afterward fit into any category.\r
\r
She wrote one line at the bottom of the table.\r
\r
*If it's not the player controlling the power, then who is?*\r
\r
---\r
\r
The profile Brawler 142536 became the eye of the storm.\r
\r
New followers by the thousands, then tens of thousands, every minute. The view counter spinning like a runaway roulette wheel.\r
\r
In elite dojos scattered across the four corners of the world, old masters stopped training mid-session. The silence of the rooms was broken by whispers in dozens of languages.\r
\r
A ghost had come out of anonymity and shattered, with half a dozen moves, everything known about that game.\r
\r
Within a few hours, Kim had crossed the distance between nobody and the center of the planet's attention.\r
\r
And he had no idea.\r
\r
He was at an abandoned complex next to the school, in Marelia, beside his best friend, offering a cigarette to a pink-haired girl.\r
`;export{e as default};
